 # Exponential Fog (Beer-Lambert)
+
 Also called: Depth Fog, Distance Fog, Volumetric Fog
 
 ## Overview
-Exponential Fog (Beer-Lambert) is a depth-cueing technique that simulates atmospheric scattering by fading scene colors toward a background (sky) color based on the viewer's distance from the camera [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visual discontinuity at the far clipping plane and hides tiling artifacts by creating a physically plausible exponential decay of visibility [S3, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Linear Distance Fog](4409-linear-distance-fog.md) by using an exponential decay function rather than a linear blend, and from [Heterogeneous Medium Transmittance — Beer's Law](4793-heterogeneous-medium-transmittance-beer-s-law.md) by assuming a constant density homogeneous medium [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
+Exponential fog is a depth-cueing technique that simulates atmospheric scattering by fading scene colors toward a background (sky) color based on the viewer's distance from the camera [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It differs from linear distance fog by using an exponential decay function, which provides a more physically plausible absorption model based on the Beer-Lambert law, and from heterogeneous models by assuming a uniform density throughout the medium [S1, S3, unofficial].
 
