 ### [Height-based Fog with Directional In-scatter](4400-height-based-fog-with-directional-in-scatter.md)
-This method extends [Exponential Fog (Beer-Lambert)](3968-exponential-fog-beer-lambert.md) by introducing altitude-dependent density falloff and directional in-scatter to simulate aerial perspective. It solves the problem of flat, uniform fog by making distant objects appear hazy and colored by the sky or sun, thereby maintaining distance perception in large-scale scenes. It differs from [Linear Distance Fog](4409-linear-distance-fog.md) by using exponential height falloff rather than linear distance blending, and from [Fog Blending with Clouds](4123-fog-blending-with-clouds.md) by focusing on atmospheric haze rather than volumetric cloud structures.
+This method simulates aerial perspective by adding a directional in-scatter term to a height-based fog model, making distant objects appear hazy and colored by the sky or sun while maintaining distance perception.
 
 ### [Linear Distance Fog](4409-linear-distance-fog.md)
-Linear distance fog is a depth-cueing technique that attenuates surface brightness linearly with view distance, fading colors toward black (or a specified floor color) to simulate atmospheric density or limited visibility. Unlike [Exponential Fog (Beer-Lambert)](3968-exponential-fog-beer-lambert.md), which models physical light absorption via an exponential decay, linear fog is a geometric approximation that is computationally cheaper and easier to implement in fixed-point or integer arithmetic. It serves as a simpler alternative to [Fog Blending with Clouds](4123-fog-blending-with-clouds.md) or [Height-based Fog with Directional In-scatter](4400-height-based-fog-with-directional-in-scatter.md) when volumetric scattering effects are not required.
+Linear distance fog is a depth-cueing technique that attenuates surface brightness linearly with view distance, fading colors toward black with a brightness floor to simulate atmospheric density or limited visibility. Unlike [Exponential Fog (Beer-Lambert)](3968-exponential-fog-beer-lambert.md), which models physical light absorption via exponential decay, linear fog is a geometric approximation implemented with integer or fixed-point arithmetic for performance.
 
